    "params": {
        "video_subject": "2 · Why rare feels rich",
        "video_script": "Number two: why rare feels rich. In a classic experiment, people were asked to taste a cookie and rate it. Some took their cookie from a jar holding ten cookies. Others from a jar holding just two. Same cookies, same recipe. The cookies from the nearly empty jar were rated as more desirable and more valuable. And it got stronger when the jar went from full to almost empty in front of people, as if others had grabbed them first. Those cookies were rated highest of all. Your brain treats scarcity as information. If only a few are left, something must be good about them, and someone else wants them too. For most of human history, that was a smart shortcut. Luxury brands understand this better than anyone: waiting lists, limited editions, only three left in stock. Think about the last time a booking site told you only two rooms were left. Did the room suddenly look nicer? The thing itself hasn't changed. The jar just looks emptier. Try this today: when you feel the pull of 'only a few left', ask yourself whether you'd still want it if the shelf were full. Scarcity changes how you see things. The next study shows how other people see you, depending on what you wear.",
